- Elevated troponin (highly specific post-trauma);  
- Sinus tachycardia and hypotension — signs of myocardial dysfunction;  
- Absence of alternative explanations (no CAD history, no ECG ischemia *yet*, no echo evidence of other structural disease).  

All eight domains are now sufficiently addressed. Though ECG remains outstanding, its absence does not invalidate the diagnosis — troponin elevation + mechanism + clinical context is sufficient for high-probability diagnosis. Continuing to chase unavailable tests would waste turns.

